2. Introduction

DevOps is a software engineering approach that combines Development (Dev) and Operations (Ops) teams to improve software development, testing and deployment.

CI/CD stands for Continuous Integration and Continuous Delivery/Deployment.

Continuous Integration (CI) involves regularly combining code changes from different developers and automatically testing them.

Continuous Delivery/Deployment (CD) automates the process of preparing and releasing software updates.

DevOps and CI/CD help software companies deliver software faster, improve quality and reduce manual work.

3. Problem Analysis

A software company develops an online shopping application and frequently updates it to introduce new features and fix bugs.

However, manual testing and deployment create several problems:

1. Manual testing consumes a lot of time.
2. Frequent updates may introduce software errors.
3. Manual deployment increases the chances of human mistakes.
4. Different development teams may face integration problems.
5. Releasing new features becomes slow and difficult.

Therefore, the company needs an automated software development and deployment process using DevOps practices and CI/CD pipelines.

4. Proposed Solution

The proposed solution is to implement DevOps practices with a CI/CD pipeline.

The pipeline automatically builds, tests and deploys the online shopping application whenever developers make changes to the source code.

A. Meaning of CI and CD

1. Continuous Integration (CI)

Continuous Integration is the process of frequently combining code changes into a shared repository.

Whenever a developer submits new code, automated tools build and test the application to identify errors.

2. Continuous Delivery/Deployment (CD)

Continuous Delivery automatically prepares tested software for release.

Continuous Deployment goes one step further by automatically releasing successfully tested changes into the production environment.

B. CI/CD Pipeline Stages

1. Code Development

Developers write code and implement new features or fix existing bugs.

2. Code Repository

Developers upload their code to a shared repository using Git and GitHub.

3. Build

The CI/CD tool automatically compiles the code and creates the application build.

4. Testing

Automated tests are performed to identify errors and verify that the application works correctly.

5. Deployment

After successful testing, the application is deployed to the staging or production environment according to the deployment process.

6. Monitoring

The application is continuously monitored to identify performance issues and errors.

C. Explanation of Build, Test and Deployment

- Build: Converts source code into an executable application or deployable package.
- Test: Checks whether the application works correctly and identifies bugs.
- Deployment: Releases the tested application so users can access the latest version.

